WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.030
This term, my class has twelve

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.310
hundred coins for outings, and

3
00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.970
five ideas on the board.

4
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:10.030
The zoo is the favourite, but

5
00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.330
it’s also the dearest.

6
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.720
How do I choose the best plan?

7
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.320
Today: what makes a choice the

8
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.700
best, and how to see it clearly.

9
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.190
Every decision like this has three parts.

10
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.240
The options: what you can choose.

11
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.050
The limits: what every plan must keep.

12
00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.930
And the goal: what “best” means, one

13
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.170
number to make as big or as small as

14
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:37.610
possible.

15
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.000
For Amina, the options are

16
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.220
the five outings.

17
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:44.620
The limit is the money:

18
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.240
twelve hundred coins.

19
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.570
The goal is the most pupils’ votes,

20
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.930
because the class voted for what they

21
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:52.130
want.

22
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.880
A plan that keeps every

23
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.700
limit is called feasible.

24
00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.730
There are usually many feasible plans.

25
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.040
The best feasible plan, the one with the

26
00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.980
best score on the goal, is called optimal.

27
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.960
Amina tries the obvious

28
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.680
rule: favourite first.

29
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.580
The zoo, seven hundred coins and

30
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.060
twenty-five votes, then the museum,

31
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.600
five hundred coins and eighteen votes.

32
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.040
That’s all twelve hundred

33
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.160
coins, and forty-three votes.

34
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.090
But the museum, the farm and the

35
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.710
river park also cost twelve hundred

36
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.530
coins, and win forty-five votes.

37
00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:35.310
Three smaller outings beat

38
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.490
one big favourite.

39
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.040
Checking a plan is easy: just add it up.

40
00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:42.810
Finding the best is the hard part.

41
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:44.660
Quick check!

42
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.850
My plan keeps every limit: the budget,

43
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.470
the dates, the seats on the bus.

44
00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.660
What does that tell me?

45
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.660
It’s feasible: allowed,

46
00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.100
but not yet the best.

47
00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.030
To know it’s optimal, you compare

48
00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.710
it with every other plan, or use

49
00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.810
a method that is sure.

50
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.520
With five outings there are only

51
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:08.980
thirty-two plans to check.

52
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.360
With thirty choices there

53
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.700
are over a billion.

54
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.400
You meet these problems all over school.

55
00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.480
The timetable: periods and

56
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.240
teachers, with no clashes.

57
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.720
Exam week: no pupil in two exams at once.

58
00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.020
The bus round: every

59
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.800
village, the shortest drive.

60
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.330
And lunch: enough food,

61
00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:33.730
for the least money.

62
00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.930
In the app, pack a truck

63
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.130
for a village school.

64
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.920
Each load has a weight and points for

65
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.240
how much the school needs it, and the

66
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.140
truck takes a thousand kilos.

67
00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.350
Try your own load first, then

68
00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:50.450
compare it with the best.

69
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.260
Your turn.

70
00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.910
A colleague says the best plan always

71
00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.310
starts with the class favourite.

72
00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:58.730
Is she right?

73
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.540
Not always: three smaller outings

74
00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.880
won more votes than the zoo.

75
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.370
Remember: name the options,

76
00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.390
the limits and the goal.

77
00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.970
A feasible plan keeps every limit; the

78
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.970
optimal one is the best of them.

79
00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:15.890
And check a quick rule

80
00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:16.990
before you trust it.

81
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.280
Try it in class tomorrow: plan

82
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.520
the class outings together.

83
00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.050
Write five ideas with their

84
00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.250
costs, and let pupils vote.

85
00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.100
Then groups find the mix with the

86
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.940
most votes inside the budget.

87
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.990
Ask them: whose favourite was left out,

88
00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.310
and what would be fair next term?
